ADDENDA <Act No. 8635, Aug. 3, 2007>
Article 1 (Enforcement date)
(4) The committee for enhancement of market efficiency established pursuant to Article 25 of the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed to be the committee for enhancement of market efficiency established under Article 414 hereof.
Article 2 (Repeal of Acts)
(1) The following laws shall be repealed:
1. Securities and Exchange Act;
2. Futures Trading Act;
3. Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act;
4. Trust Business Act;
5. Merchant Banks Act;
6. Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act.
Article 3 (Matters concerning the establishment of the Korea Financial Investment Association)
(1) The Korea Financial Investment Association (hereinafter referred to as the "Association") shall be the Korea Securities Dealers Association established pursuant to Article 162 of the former Securities and Exchange Act, the Futures Association established with permission pursuant to Article 75 of the former Futures Trading Act, and the former Futures Trading Act; The Asset Management Association established with permission under Article 160(3) of the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act (hereinafter referred to as "association subject to merger") shall be established by a merger.
(2) The Establishment Committee of the Korea Financial Investment Association (hereinafter referred to as the "Establishment Committee") shall be established to deal with affairs concerning the merger and the establishment of the Association under paragraph (1).
(3) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Establishment Committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) The Establishment Committee may request the association subject to merger to provide human and material support necessary for the establishment of the association.
(5) The associations subject to merger shall prepare a written merger contract stating the matters prescribed by Presidential Decree and obtain approval by a majority of the total voting rights at a general meeting of members.
(6) Within one week from the date on which a general meeting of members adopts a resolution for approval under paragraph (5), the association subject to merger shall give public notice that creditors are required to raise an objection within a period of not less than two weeks if they have an objection to the merger, and the creditors known to the association subject to merger shall be notified publicly: A peremptory notice of such fact shall be given.
(7) Article 232(2) and (3) of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the public announcement and peremptory notice under paragraph (6).
(8) Where the procedures referred to in paragraphs (6) and (7) are completed, the establishment committee shall convene an inaugural general meeting of the Association without delay.
(9) Articles 309, 311(1), 312 and 316 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the inaugural general meeting under paragraph (8). In such cases, "incorporators" in Article 311(1) of the same Act shall be construed as "chairperson of the Establishment Committee."
(10) The Establishment Committee shall prepare an application for approval of merger and the articles of association of the Association and obtain approval from the Financial Services Commission. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(11) An application for approval of a merger under paragraph (10) shall include the following matters, and shall be accompanied by a merger contract and business-related regulations:
1. Name of the association;
2. Domiciles of the central association and branch associations;
3. Name, resident registration number, and address of executives;
4.Trade name or title of members;
(12) Upon obtaining approval under paragraph (10), the Establishment Committee shall register the incorporation of the Association without delay.
(13) A merger shall take effect upon registration of the establishment of the Association under paragraph (12). In such cases, the Association subject to merger shall cease to exist simultaneously with the establishment of the Association without undergoing liquidation procedures.
(14) Where the establishment committee completes the registration of incorporation under paragraph (12), it shall transfer its administrative affairs to the chairperson of the Association.
(15) The establishment commissioners shall, when the transfer of its business affairs and assets pursuant to paragraph (4) has been completed, be deemed to have been decommissioned.
Article 4 (Applicability to investment recommendation)
Articles 46 through 48 shall begin to apply from the first investment recommendation made after this Act enters into force.
Article 5 (Special cases concerning authorization and registration of financial investment business by report)
(1) With respect to a person who engages in a business equivalent to any of the subparagraphs of Article 6(1) as at the date one year has passed after the promulgation of this Act, the requirements for the maintenance of authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20 within the scope of the business in which he or she is engaged; A report may be filed with the Financial Services Commission within two months from the date on which one year has elapsed after the promulgation of this Act. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (1), the Financial Services Commission shall verify whether the reporter meets the requirements for the maintenance of authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20, and shall notify the reporter of the results thereof by the day immediately before the date this Act enters into force. In such cases, a person who has been notified that he or she meets the requirements for the maintenance of authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20 shall be deemed to have obtained authorization for the financial investment business or registered for the financial investment business on the date this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(3) Notwithstanding Articles 11 and 17, six months after this Act enters into force, even where a person who has filed a report pursuant to paragraph (1) is notified pursuant to paragraph (2) that he or she has failed to meet the requirements for the maintenance of authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20; Until then, the business operator may continue to engage in the business previously conducted. In such cases, a financial investment business entity shall be deemed a financial investment business entity under this Act to the extent that it conducts its business.
(4) A person who has been notified that he or she has failed to meet the requirements for the maintenance of authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20 from among reporters notified pursuant to paragraph (2) may file a report again with the Financial Services Commission within three months from the date this Act enters into force. . <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(5) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (4), the Financial Services Commission shall ascertain whether the reporter meets the requirements for maintaining authorization under Article 15 or the requirements for maintaining registration under Article 20 and shall notify the reporting person of the results thereof within six months from the date this Act enters into force. Do; <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 6 (Special cases concerning authorization and registration of financial investment business following additional business units)
(1) Where a person who is engaged in a business equivalent to any of the subparagraphs of Article 6(1) as at one year after the date of the promulgation of this Act intends to add an authorized business unit or a registered business unit to the business he or she is engaged in, he or she shall Within two months after the date of the promulgation of the Act, a person may apply for authorization for a financial investment business or registration of a financial investment business by integrating the business he or she has been engaged in and the business unit he or she intends to add.
(2) Upon receipt of an application for authorization or registration pursuant to paragraph (1), the Financial Services Commission shall examine the details thereof and notify the claimant of the results thereof by the day before the date this Act enters into force. In such cases, Articles 12 and 13 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the requirements, application, examination, etc. of authorization, and Articles 18 and 19 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the requirements, application, and examination of registration. Provided, That the requirements for authorization and the requirements for registration under Article 15 and the requirements for the maintenance of registration under Article 20 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the requirements for authorization and registration of the business being operated in the past. <Amended by Presidential Decree No. 21307, Feb. 29, 2008>
(3) Notwithstanding Articles 11 and 17, a person who has applied for authorization or registration pursuant to paragraph (1) shall continue to conduct the business he or she has been engaged in until six months after this Act enters into force, notwithstanding Articles 11 and 17, even if authorization or registration is rejected pursuant to paragraph (2). May be. In such cases, a financial investment business entity shall be deemed a financial investment business entity under this Act to the extent that it conducts its business.
(4) An claimant notified pursuant to paragraph (2), whose authorization or registration has been rejected, may file an application again with the Financial Services Commission within three months from the date this Act enters into force, satisfying the requirements under paragraph (2). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(5) Upon receipt of an application for authorization or registration under paragraph (4), the Financial Services Commission shall examine or examine whether the requirements for authorization or registration under paragraph (2) are met, and notify the claimant of the results thereof within six months from the date this Act enters into force. . <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 7 (General transitional measures)
(1) The former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Merchant Bank Act, or the former Korea Securities as at the time this Act enters into force; Futures Exchange Act; An act shall be deemed an act; <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) The former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Merchant Bank Act, or the former Korea Securities as at the time this Act enters into force; Reports, applications, reports, and other acts done to the Financial Services Commission, the Securities and Futures Commission, or the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service pursuant to the Futures Exchange Act shall be deemed to have been made to the Financial Services Commission, the Securities and Futures Commission, or the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service under this Act. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 8 (Transitional measures concerning qualifications, etc. of executive officers of financial investment business entities) <Amended on February 3, 2009>
(1) Article 24 (including cases applicable mutatis mutandis in Articles 289, 301(4), 327(2), 382, and 402(6)) to the qualifications for executive officers of financial investment business entities in office as at the time this Act enters into force; The former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, or the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act."
(2) The provisions of subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Articles 289, 301(4), 327(2), 382, and 402(6)); In applying this Act, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
(3) With respect to an executive of a financial investment business entity who holds office concurrently as at the time this Act enters into force, who concurrently holds office in violation of Article 45(2)2, the expiration date of his or her term of office as an executive of the financial investment business entity and the term of office of an executive of another company in which he or she concurrently holds office; Article 45(2)2 shall not apply until the expiration date, whichever comes first. <Added on Feb. 3, 2009>
Article 9 (Transitional measures concerning appointment of outside directors and composition of board of directors of financial investment business entities)
A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs and is required to appoint a new outside director pursuant to Article 25 after this Act enters into force shall appoint an outside director pursuant to the same Article by the date of the first regular general meeting of stockholders convened after this Act enters into force: In such cases, a person appointed as an outside director at a general meeting of shareholders shall be deemed recommended by the outside director candidate recommendation committee pursuant to Article 25(2) and (4).
1. A futures company under the former Futures Trading Act;
2. An investment advisory company under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act;
3. A trust company under the former Trust Business Act;
Article 10 (Transitional measures concerning establishment of audit committee of financial investment business entities)
A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs and is required to establish a new audit committee pursuant to Article 26 as at the time this Act enters into force shall establish an audit committee pursuant to the same Article by the date of the first regular general meeting of stockholders convened after this Act enters into force:
1. A futures company under the former Futures Trading Act;
2. An investment advisory company under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act;
3. A trust company under the former Trust Business Act;
Article 11 (Transitional measures concerning appointment of standing auditor of financial investment business entities)
A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs and is required to newly appoint a full-time auditor pursuant to Article 27 as this Act enters into force shall appoint a full-time auditor pursuant to the same Article by the date of the first regular general meeting of shareholders convened after this Act enters into force:
1. A futures company under the former Futures Trading Act;
2. A futures company under the former Futures Trading Act;
3. An asset management company and an investment advisory company under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act;
4. A trust company under the former Trust Business Act;
Article 12 (Transitional measures concerning compliance officer)
(1) A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs and is required to newly appoint a compliance officer pursuant to Article 28 after this Act enters into force shall appoint a compliance officer pursuant to the same Article within one month after this Act enters into force:
1. A futures company under the former Futures Trading Act;
2. An investment advisory company under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act;
3. A trust company under the former Trust Business Act;
(2) Notwithstanding Article 28(4), with respect to a compliance officer who is in office or in office under the former Securities and Exchange Act or the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force, until his or her term of office expires (in cases of employees); The requirements prescribed in the former Securities and Exchange Act or the former Act on Business of Operating Indirect Investment and Assets shall apply.
(3) In applying Article 28(4)1d, the careers of executive officers and employees of the association subject to merger shall be deemed included in the careers of the Association.
(4) In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 under Article 28(4)2, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
(5) For the purpose of Article 28(4)3, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, and the former Korea The Securities and Futures Exchange Act and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
Article 13 (Transitional measures concerning maintenance of financial soundness)
Where a securities company falling under any subparagraph of Article 3 of the Addenda of the amended Securities and Exchange Act (Act No. 6176) becomes an investment trader or investment broker pursuant to Articles 5 and 6 of the Addenda to this Act, the requirements for maintaining financial soundness: Article 30(1) shall not apply until the date prescribed in Article 3 of the Addenda of the amended Securities and Exchange Act (Act No. 6176).
Article 14 (Transitional measures concerning report of incidental business of financial investment business entities)
Notwithstanding the latter part of Article 40 and Article 41(1), a person who engages in incidental business affairs under 41(1) pursuant to subparagraph 2 or 5 of Article 40 pursuant to the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, and the former Trust Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force may file a report with the Financial Services Commission within one month from the date this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 15 (Transitional measures against solicitors for acquisition of indirect investment securities)
Notwithstanding Articles 51 and 52, collective investment securities shall apply to persons who meet the requirements to be entrusted with the solicitation of acquisition of indirect investment securities pursuant to the former Act on the Business of Operating Indirect Investment Assets as at the time this Act enters into force, for up to one month from the date this Act enters into force. A recommendation for acquisition may be entrusted. In such cases, a person entrusted with the solicitation of acquisition of collective investment securities may make an investment recommendation, notwithstanding Article 51(2).
Article 16 (Transitional measures for similar investment advisory business entities)
A person who has reported quasi-investment advisory business pursuant to Article 149 of the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed to have reported quasi-investment advisory business pursuant to Article 101.
Article 17 (Transitional measures concerning accounting audit of trusts)
`Articles 114 and 115 shall not apply to a trust established in accordance with terms and conditions or standardized contracts enacted or amended before the enforcement date of the amended Trust Business Act (Act No. 6180). Provided, That Articles 114 and 115 shall apply to another trust established in accordance with terms and conditions or standardized contracts enacted or amended after the enforcement date of the amended Trust Business Act (Act No. 6180).
Article 18 (Transitional measures concerning securities registration statement)
Notwithstanding Articles 118 through 132, the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to a securities registration statement, a blanket statement, a corrective statement, a prospectus (including a preliminary prospectus and a short prospectus) and a report on the results of issuance of securities submitted to the Financial Services Commission pursuant to the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 19 (Transitional measures concerning report on treasury stocks and merger)
Notwithstanding Articles 118 through 132 and 161 through 165, where the duty to report has arisen pursuant to Article 189-2 of the former Securities and Exchange Act and Article 190-2 of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force, the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply.
Article 20 (Transitional measures concerning tender offer statement)
Notwithstanding Articles 133 through 146, the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to the tender offer statement, the corrective statement, the prospectus for tender offer, the tender offer withdrawal statement, and the public notice of the tender offer and the corrective notice submitted pursuant to the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force.
Article 21 (Transitional measures concerning report on holding, etc. of stocks in bulk)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 147(1), where a person exempted from reporting obligations under Article 200-2(1) of the former Securities and Exchange Act who holds stocks, etc. in large quantities under Article 200-2(1) shall file a report under Article 147(1) as at the time this Act enters into force no later than one month from the date this Act enters into force.
(2) Any person who has reported in accordance with Article 200-2(1) of the previous Securities Exchange Act as at the time of enforcement of this Act and who has changed important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the details of major contracts regarding stocks held, etc., shall be file a report under Article 147(4) no later than one month from the date of enforcement of this Act.
(3) Article 148 shall not apply where the obligation to report arises pursuant to Article 200-2(1) or (4) of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force.
(4) Notwithstanding Article 150(1), where the duty to report arises pursuant to Article 200-2(4) of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force, Article 200 of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to restrictions, etc. on the exercise of voting rights of violating stocks, etc.
(5) If the obligation to report that the purpose of holding stocks, etc. is to influence the management rights of the issuer arises pursuant to Article 200-2(1), (3) or (4) of the previous Securities Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force, Article 150(3) shall not apply.
Article 22 (Transitional measures concerning solicitation to exercise voting rights by proxy)
Notwithstanding the amended Articles 152-2 and 153, where a notice or announcement of calling a general meeting of shareholders has been given as at the time this Act enters into force, solicitations to exercise voting rights by proxy with respect to such general meeting of shareholders shall be governed by the former Securities Exchange Act.
Article 23 (Transitional measures concerning occasional public disclosure and business report)
Notwithstanding Articles 159 through 165, where the duty to report arises pursuant to Article 186 of the former Securities and Exchange Act or the duty to submit arises pursuant to Articles 186-2 and 186-3 of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force, the previous Securities and Exchange Act shall apply.
Article 24 (Transitional measures concerning restriction on ownership of shares issued by public purpose corporations)
Notwithstanding Article 167(1)1, the previous Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to the ownership of stocks of stockholders who fall under Article 200(1)1 of the previous Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force.
Article 25 (Transitional measures concerning auditors' liabilities for damage)
Notwithstanding Article 170, Article 197 of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to the liability of an auditor who has conducted an accounting audit pursuant to Article 194-3 of the former Securities and Exchange Act.
Article 26 (Transitional measures concerning return of short-term trading profits by insiders)
Notwithstanding the provisions of Article 172(1) through (3), Articles 188(2) through (4) shall apply to an executive officer or employee of a stock-listed corporation or a KOSDAQ-listed corporation or a major shareholder under the former Securities and Exchange Act purchases or sells stock certificates, etc. before this Act enters into force and obtains profits by selling or purchasing such stock certificates, etc. within six months after this Act enters into force, Article 188(2) through (4) of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall apply to claims for the return of profits, standards for calculation, procedures for return, etc.
Article 27 (Transitional measures concerning report on status of ownership of specific securities)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 173(1), the deadline for reporting ownership of a person who owns specific securities, etc. (excluding a person who owns only stocks) at the time this Act enters into force, who is an executive officer or major shareholder of a stock-listed corporation or a KOSDAQ-listed corporation under the former Securities and Exchange Act shall be one month from the date this Act enters into force.
(2) Where an executive or a major shareholder of a stock-listed corporation or a KOSDAQ-listed corporation under the former Securities and Exchange Act, who owns only stocks as at the time this Act enters into force, has changed the number of stocks held before this Act enters into force, Article 188(6) of the Securities and Exchange Act shall apply.
Article 28 (Transitional measures concerning indirect investment schemes)
(1) An investment trust (excluding a special account created by an insurance company) or an investment company created or established in accordance with the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be governed by the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act.
(2) The special account of an insurance company under Article 135(1) of the previous Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed the special account of an insurance company under Article 251. In such cases, if the trust contract of the special account violates this Act, the insurance company operating the special account shall change the trust contract of the special account in compliance with this Act within three months after this Act enters into force.
(3) A private equity fund registered under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed a private equity fund registered under this Act.
(4) Foreign indirect investment securities registered with the Financial Services Commission in accordance with the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be governed by the former Enforcement Decree of the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act.
(5) The former Securities Investment Trust Business Act or the former Securities Investment Company Act shall apply to securities investment trusts and securities investment companies under the proviso to Article 2(1) of the Addenda to the Act on Business of Operating Indirect Investment and Assets (Act No. 6987).
(6) Money trusts and special accounts under Article 14(2) of Addenda of the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act of Act (No. 6987) shall be governed by the provisions of this Act on trust business or the Insurance Business Act.
(7) Real estate investment companies (excluding self-managed real estate investment companies), ship investment companies, companies specializing in cultural industries, corporate restructuring associations, and investment in the establishment of small and medium enterprises, new technology venture business investment associations, created or established pursuant to any of the following Acts as at the time this Act enters into force: This Act shall not apply to associations, Korea Venture Funds, Korea Venture Investment Associations, individual investment associations, specialized investment association for components and materials, and corporate restructuring investment companies: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2009>
1. Deleted; <Feb. 3, 2009>
2. The Real Estate Investment Company Act;
3. The Ship Investment Company Act;
4. The Framework Act on the Promotion of Cultural Industries;
5. The Industrial Development Act;
6. The Support for Small and Medium Enterprise Establishment Act;
7. The Specialized Credit Financial Business Act;
8. The Act on Special Measures for the Promotion of Venture Businesses;
9. Act on Special Measures for the Promotion of Specialized Enterprises for Components and Materials;
10. Deleted; <Feb. 3, 2009>
11. The former Corporate Restructuring Investment Companies Act.
Article 29 (Transitional measures concerning conversion into indirect investment schemes)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 28(1) of the Addenda, a collective investment business entity or investment company that manages the property of an investment trust (excluding a special account managed by an insurance company; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) created under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act may register such investment trust or investment company with the Financial Services Commission as a collective investment scheme under this Act pursuant to Article 182. In such cases, it shall submit a registration statement to the Financial Services Commission pursuant to Article 119(1) and (2). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) Notwithstanding Article 28(4) of the Addenda, a collective investment business entity of a foreign investment trust or a foreign investment company that has issued foreign indirect investment securities reported to the Financial Services Commission under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, may register the foreign investment trust or foreign investment company with the Financial Services Commission as a foreign collective investment scheme under this Act pursuant to Article 279. In such cases, a registration statement shall be submitted to the Financial Services Commission pursuant to Article 119(1) and (2). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 30 (Transitional measures concerning sales of indirect investment securities)
(1) A sales company under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act shall not sell indirect investment securities (including foreign indirect investment securities reported to the Financial Services Commission) under the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act from the date on which three months elapse after this Act enters into force. Provided, That this shall not apply to cases prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) A sales company under the previous Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act shall not sell beneficiary certificates of securities investment trusts or stocks of securities investment companies under Article 11 of Addenda to the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act (Act No. 6987). Provided, this shall not apply to cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) No financial institutions under the Banking Act and insurance companies under the Insurance Business Act deemed to have obtained permission for asset management business pursuant to Article 14 of the Addenda to the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act (Act No. 6987) shall be allowed to additionally create money trust or special account created before the Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act (Act No. 6987). Provided, That this shall not apply to cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
Article 31 (Transitional measures concerning qualifications of promoters of investment company)
In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 provided in Articles 194(1) and 199(4) 1, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
Article 32 (Transitional measures concerning companies related to collective investment scheme and executive officers thereof)
(1) A general office management company, indirect investment scheme rating company, and bond rating company registered with the Financial Services Commission pursuant to Articles 25, 154, and 155 of the previous Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed a general office management company, a collective investment scheme rating company, and a bond rating company registered pursuant to Articles 254, 258, and 263 respectively. In such cases, the registration maintenance requirements under Article 254(8), Article 258(8), and Article 263(8) must be met within three months from the date of enforcement of this Act. <Amended on 2008. 2. 29.>
(2) Notwithstanding Articles 254(2) 5, 258(2)6, and 263(2)6, the qualifications for an executive in office of a general office management company, indirect investment scheme rating company, and bond rating company as at the time this Act enters into force shall be governed by the previous Indirect Investment Management Act until the office expires.
(4) In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 under Article 28(4)2 provided in Articles 254(2) 5, 268(2)6, and 263(2)6, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included in "this Act".
Article 33 (Transitional measures for investment purpose companies)
An investment purpose company under the previous Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act as at the time this Act enters into force may change the company's registered information pursuant to Article 271(5) after the enforcement of this Act.
Article 34 (Transitional measures concerning Korea Securities Depository)
(1) The Korea Securities Depository established under Article 173 of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall be deemed the Korea Securities Depository under Article 294.
(2) Securities for deposit designated by the Korea Securities Depository pursuant to Article 173-7 of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall be deemed to have been designated as securities, etc. for deposit by the Korea Securities Depository pursuant to Article 308.
(3) A client account book under Article 174-2 of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall be deemed an investor's account book under Article 310(1).
(4) Approval granted by the Securities Depository pursuant to Article 176-2(4) of the former Securities and Exchange Act shall be deemed approval granted by the Korea Securities Depository pursuant to Article 322(4).
Article 35 (Transitional measures against securities finance companies)
(1) A securities finance company that has obtained permission from the Financial Services Commission under Article 145 of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed a securities finance company authorized under Article 324(1). In such cases, the requirements for maintenance of authorization under Article 324(9) shall be met within three months from the date this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 under Article 28(4)2 provided in Article 324(2)5, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
Article 35 (Transitional measures against merchant banks)
(1) Where a merchant bank exceeds the limit on investment in securities under Article 344(1) as a result of the enforcement of this Act, it shall comply with paragraph (1) of the same Article within one year after this Act enters into force.
(2) Notwithstanding Article 24, which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350, the previous Merchant Banks Act shall apply to the qualifications for executive officers of a merchant bank in office as at the time this Act enters into force until their term of office expires.
(3) In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 under Article 28(4)2, which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included therein.
(4) A merchant bank that shall appoint outside directors pursuant to Article 25(1) which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350 shall appoint outside directors pursuant to that paragraph of that Article by the date of the first regular general meeting of stockholders convened after this Act enters into force.
(5) A merchant bank that shall appoint outside directors pursuant to Article 26(2) which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350 shall appoint outside directors pursuant to that paragraph of that Article by the date of the first regular general meeting of stockholders convened after this Act enters into force.
(6) Notwithstanding Article 28(4), which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350, the requirements prescribed in the previous Merchant Bank Act shall apply to a compliance officer of a merchant bank who holds office or is in office pursuant to the previous Merchant Banks Act as at the time this Act enters into force until his or her term of office expires (in cases of employees, until three years after this Act enters into force).
(7) For the purpose of Article 28(4)3, which is applied mutatis mutandis to Article 350, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, and the former Korea The Securities and Futures Exchange Act and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included in "this Act".
Article 37 (Transitional measures against fund brokerage companies)
(1) A fund brokerage company established with the approval of the Financial Services Commission pursuant to Article 9(1) of the previous Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed a fund brokerage company authorized under Article 355(1). In such cases, the requirements for maintaining authorization under Article 355(9) must be met within three months from the date of enforcement of this Act. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
(2) Notwithstanding Article 355(2)5, the previous Merchant Banks Act shall apply to the qualifications for executive officers of a merchant bank in office as at the time this Act enters into force until their term of office expires.
(3) In applying subparagraphs 3 and 5 through 7 of Article 24 under Article 28(4)2 provided in Article 355(2)5, the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, and the former The Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act, and the former Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to be included in "this Act".
Article 38 (Transitional measures concerning concurrent operation of short-term financial business by financial institutions)
A financial institution that has obtained authorization from the Financial Services Commission pursuant to Article 3-2(1) of the previous Merchant Banks Act shall be deemed to have obtained authorization from the Financial Services Commission for short-term financial business pursuant to Article 360(1). In such cases, the requirements for maintenance of authorization under paragraph (9) of that Article shall be met within three months from the date this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 39 (Transitional measures against transfer agents)
A transfer agency registered with the Financial Services Commission under Article 180 of the former Securities and Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed a transfer agency registered under Article 365(1). In such cases, the requirements for maintenance of registration under Article 365(8) shall be met within three months from the date this Act enters into force. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
Article 40 (Transitional measures concerning the korea exchange)
(1) The Korea Securities and Futures Exchange under the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed the Korea Exchange under Article 373.
(2) Transactions and futures transactions of securities which have been made in the securities market, the KOSDAQ market, and the futures market established by the Korea Securities and Futures Exchange under the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force and the settlement of which has not been completed shall be deemed to have been made under the same conditions in the securities market, the KOSDAQ market and the derivatives market established by the Korea Exchange established pursuant to the Act.
(3) The Joint Compensation Fund for breach of contract established pursuant to Article 95(1) of the former Securities and Exchange Act or Article 27(1) of the former Futures Trading Act shall be deemed the Joint Compensation Fund established pursuant to Article 394.
(4) The Market Efficiency Committee established under Article 25 of the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed the Market Efficiency Committee under Article 414.
Article 41 (Transitional measures concerning penalty provisions)
(1) The previous provisions shall apply to penalty provisions and administrative fines for violations of the former Securities and Exchange Act, the former Futures Trading Act, the former Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the former Trust Business Act, the former Merchant Banks Act, and the former Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act before this Act enters into force.
(2) The previous provisions shall apply to the imposition of penalty surcharge and administrative dispositions against a violation which ended before this Act enters into force or which continues after this Act enters into force, committed under the previous Securities Transaction Act, the previous Futures Trading Act, the previous Indirect Investment Asset Management Business Act, the previous Trust Business Act, the previous Merchant Banks Act and the previous Korea Securities and Futures Exchange Act before the enforcement of this Act.
Article 42 Omitted.

